    includes: ["Offer type: money, gift in kind, or both", "Components: fee, commission, bonus, prize, gifted product, discount, access", "Structure: flat, per sale, or tiered", "How they get it, when they're paid", "Optional: the Brieflee free-feedback perk"],
    worked: `<p><b>Offer type:</b> Money, with a gifted product.</p><p><b>The deal:</b> 15% commission on every Volcanic Roller sold through your TikTok Shop link, a full product set gifted to film with, and a rising rate: hit 50 sales in a month and you move to 20%, 100 and you move to 25%. Top seller each month takes a \u00a3150 bonus.</p><p><b>Structure:</b> performance, tiered by monthly sales.</p><p><b>How you get it:</b> your unique TikTok Shop link, paid monthly once sales clear the return window.</p><p><b>Free feedback:</b> drop your video into Brieflee before you post for free AI feedback on what's working and anything that might get flagged.</p>`,
    placeholder: `<p><b>Offer type:</b> Money [+ gifted product]</p><p><b>The deal:</b> [X]% commission on every sale through your TikTok Shop link[, a product set gifted, and a rising rate: hit [N] sales and move to [Y]%]. [Top seller each month takes a \u00a3[amount] bonus.]</p><p><b>Structure:</b> performance[, tiered by monthly sales].</p><p><b>How you get it:</b> your unique link, paid monthly once sales clear the return window.</p><p><b>Free feedback:</b> drop your video into Brieflee before you post for free AI feedback.</p>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Offer section of a creator brief for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Every offer is money and/or gift in kind, most stack. Match to type: Affiliate leads with commission per sale; Influencer with a flat fee per deliverable; UGC with a flat fee per video; Customer with a gift in kind; Employee Ambassador with vouchers/perks. Optionally add the Brieflee free-feedback perk, framed as a benefit not a rule. Write ONE offer with real numbers, never options to choose between. Usage rights, paid ads rights and exclusivity belong in Rights and restrictions, never here. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 3, name: "Audience / Customer", icon: "users", format: "text",
    what: "The person on the other end of the camera. Who they are, what they care about, the problem in their life, and how the creator should hold themselves toward them.",
    why: "A creator who can picture exactly who they're talking to makes content that lands as a recommendation, not an advert. Without it they perform to no one, or sell too hard.",
    includes: ["Who they follow, where they spend time, whose taste they trust", "The problem, pitched at their real awareness level (often normalized background friction)", "The creator's stance: the friend who found it first, not a spokesperson"],
    worked: `<p>You're talking to someone who follows skincare and makeup creators, saves get-ready-with-me videos, and cares about their face looking fresh from morning to night. They're the friend everyone asks for product recommendations. They already know the shine problem: by midday their T-zone is greasy, their makeup starts to slide, and blotting with tissue just lifts the makeup off with it. But they've made peace with it, so they've never gone looking for a fix. Your job is to be the person who shows them there is one. Not by selling at them, but by being genuinely excited about how your skin still looks hours in. You're not an advert, you're the friend who found the thing and can't stop talking about it.</p>`,
    placeholder: `<p>You're talking to someone who follows [the creators and world this audience lives in], [a habit that shows their taste], and cares about [what they value]. They're [their role in their circle]. They already know [the problem]: [how it actually shows up, in concrete detail]. But they've made peace with it, so they've never gone looking for a fix. Your job is to be the person who shows them there is one. Not by selling at them, but by being genuinely enthusiastic about [the better state the product gives you]. You're not an advert, you're the friend who found the thing and can't stop talking about it.</p>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Audience section of a creator brief for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Present tense, second person, to the creator about who they're talking to. Three moves: paint who the audience is; name the problem at their real awareness level (usually normalized background friction, in concrete physical detail); set the creator's stance as the friend who found it first, not a spokesperson. One tight paragraph. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 4, name: "Product / Feature", icon: "package", format: "text",
    what: "The product itself, said plainly. What it is, what it does, and the handful of things it's known for. The creator's factual reference.",
    why: "A creator can't talk credibly about something they don't understand. This is the ground truth they build from.",
    includes: ["What the product is, in plain terms", "What it's designed to do or replace", "The two or three USPs it's known for"],
    worked: `<p>The Volcanic Roller is a reusable oil-absorbing tool designed to replace disposable blotting papers. Made from real volcanic stone, it lifts excess oil off your skin in seconds without touching your makeup, and a quick wash brings it back to new, so one roller lasts where a pack of papers runs out. It's known for being reusable, mess-free, and kind to both your skin and the planet.</p>`,
    placeholder: `<p>[Product] is a [category, in plain terms] designed to [what it does or replaces]. Made from [material or method], it [core benefit, how it works] without [the usual trade-off], and [a second USP that sets it apart]. It's known for being [two or three qualities it's recognized for].</p>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Product section of a creator brief for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Present tense, third person, plain and factual, free of hype. Cover: what the product is (its category), what it does and who for, and the two or three qualities it's genuinely known for. Descriptive not persuasive. Two or three sentences. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 5, name: "Creator Benefits", icon: "gift", format: "bulletList",
    what: "Why the creator personally wins. For commission-led creators the money case first; for fee-led creators, the benefits beyond the fee.",
    why: "A creator sizing up a brief they've never worked on needs to believe it's worth their effort. Land that and they're in.",
    includes: ["The earning case (commission-led): proof it converts, and the brand boosts their best videos", "The content win: what the product does for their videos", "The personal win: how it fits their life", "The growth win: boosting, whitelisting, repeat work, access"],
    worked: `<p><b>Why creators love it:</b></p><ul><li><b>This one sells:</b> the Volcanic Roller is our top mover on TikTok Shop, with videos regularly passing six figures in views, converting because the result is visible on camera. When it flies, your commission flies with it.</li><li><b>We back your best:</b> we actively boost your top-performing videos to put them in front of new audiences, so a good video keeps earning long after you post it.</li><li><b>The wow factor:</b> an instant on-camera before and after that stops the scroll and does half the selling for you.</li><li><b>Your travel bestie:</b> the go-to tool for staying matte on the go, no more bathroom trips blotting oil with loo roll.</li></ul>`,
    placeholder: `<p><b>Why creators love it:</b></p><ul><li><b>This one sells:</b> [proof it converts, e.g. top mover, visible result], and when it flies your commission flies with it.</li><li><b>[The growth win]:</b> [how the brand compounds earnings, e.g. we boost your best videos].</li><li><b>[The content win]:</b> [what it does on camera].</li><li><b>[The personal win]:</b> [how it fits their life].</li></ul>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Creator Benefits section for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator whose offer is [OFFER TYPE: commission-led or fee-led]. If commission-led, open with the earning case (proof it sells, and the brand compounds it by boosting), money first. If fee-led, lead with benefits beyond the fee (content, ease of shooting, portfolio, fit, repeat work). Three or four short benefits, each a bold label with one line under. Warm, concrete. No em dashes."
  },

  {
