    worked: `<p><b>Output:</b> 3 videos, each with a different hook. 20 to 30 seconds. Output sizes: 9:16.</p><p><b>Delivery:</b> final edited video in your signature style, plus raw footage for additional usage. Channels: TikTok, Meta.</p><p><b>Timing:</b> submissions open on receipt of product, close 10 days later.</p>`,
    placeholder: `<p><b>Output:</b> [number] video(s)[, each with a different hook / plus a polished cut and a raw cut]. [duration]. Output sizes: [9:16 / 1:1 / 16:9].</p><p><b>Delivery:</b> [final edited video / raw footage / both]. Channels: [channels].</p><p><b>Timing:</b> [submissions open [date], close [date] / posting window is [window]].</p>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Deliverables section for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Plain spec grouped into output, delivery, timing. UGC is a file handover on submission dates; Influencers/Affiliates/Employees post native with a posting window. Real numbers over vague ranges. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 7, name: "Hook", icon: "zap", format: "table",
    what: "The opening three seconds, built as three components that fire together: caption on screen, visual action on camera, first spoken words. Options to test.",
    why: "Three seconds decides whether the rest gets watched. The single biggest lever on whether content performs.",
    includes: ["Caption hook: on-screen text, works on mute", "Visual action hook: the first 1\u20132 seconds on camera", "Voiceover hook: the first words spoken, oddly specific", "Awareness stage + psychological trigger"],
    worked: `<p><b>Hook 1 (warm, demo led)</b></p><ul><li><b>Caption:</b> This removes oil without ruining your makeup</li><li><b>Visual action:</b> roll the stone down the T-zone, oil visibly lifting off</li><li><b>Voiceover:</b> \u201cWatch how much oil this pulls off my face.\u201d</li></ul><p><b>Hook 2 (cold, call-out)</b></p><ul><li><b>Caption:</b> If your face gets shiny by noon, you need this</li><li><b>Visual action:</b> close on a shiny T-zone in daylight, then the first roll</li><li><b>Voiceover:</b> \u201cOily skin girls, this one's for you.\u201d</li></ul><p><b>Hook 3 (warm, confession)</b></p><ul><li><b>Caption:</b> I stopped using blotting papers because of this</li><li><b>Visual action:</b> pick the roller up like you're not convinced</li><li><b>Voiceover:</b> \u201cI didn't think this would work until I tried it.\u201d</li></ul>`,
    placeholder: `<p><b>Hook 1</b></p><ul><li><b>Caption:</b> [on-screen text, works on mute]</li><li><b>Visual action:</b> [what's on camera in the first 1 to 2 seconds]</li><li><b>Voiceover:</b> [the first words, oddly specific]</li></ul><p>Add Hook 2 and Hook 3 the same way, varied across types.</p>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Hook section for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Creator-led only (brand-led hooks live in the Script). Three components together: caption (works on mute), visual action (first 1\u20132s), voiceover (first words). Choose by persona, then format, then awareness stage. Caption and voiceover complement, never duplicate. Oddly specific beats vague. Offer several varied options. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 8, name: "Talking Points", icon: "message-square", format: "bulletList",
    what: "Talking Points and Script are the same slot filled two ways, never both. Creator-led uses Talking Points; brand-led uses Script.",
    why: "Left to guess, a creator hits generic points that convince no one. Give them the angles that matter, without scripting every word.",
    includes: ["The buying trigger, made specific", "The honest hesitation", "The proof: material, build, guarantee", "The before and after", "A soft, conversational CTA"],
    worked: `<p><b>Talking points to hit</b></p><ul><li><b>What finally made you try it?</b> The midday shine, the makeup sliding, blotting with loo roll and lifting half your makeup off with it. Make it specific and personal.</li><li><b>What were you skeptical about?</b> Maybe you thought a stone couldn't do much, or that it'd shift your makeup. Be honest about the hesitation.</li><li><b>The material and how it works.</b> Real volcanic stone, reusable and washable, not a disposable you keep rebuying. Lifts oil, leaves makeup.</li><li><b>How your day actually changed.</b> Still matte at 3pm, no bathroom trips, one small tool that lives in your bag.</li><li><b>Soft CTA.</b> Land on something like \u201cif you're still blotting with loo roll, just try this.\u201d Conversational, not salesy.</li></ul>`,
    placeholder: `<p><b>Talking points to hit</b></p><ul><li><b>What finally made you try it?</b> [the trigger and pain, made specific]. Make it personal.</li><li><b>What were you skeptical about?</b> [the honest hesitation]. Be honest.</li><li><b>The material and how it works.</b> [build, mechanism, any guarantee].</li><li><b>How your day actually changed.</b> [the before and after, in real detail].</li><li><b>Soft CTA.</b> [conversational close].</li></ul>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Talking Points section for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Creator-led only (brand-led uses Script). Second person, present tense, prompts that pull the right things out of them, not a word-for-word script. Story-led types (Customer, Influencer) get questions that draw out real experience; sell-led types (Affiliate, UGC) get the actual selling points and lines. About five points, each a clear direction. Keep the honest hesitation in. No em dashes."
  },

  {
    n: 9, name: "Script", icon: "file-text", format: "table",
    what: "The brand-led counterpart to Talking Points. You own the words, the creator performs them. Written as a storyboard, scene by scene.",
    why: "For brand-led content, a storyboard puts the beats in order, scripts the lines that must land, and places on-screen text where it sells, so the cut comes back right first time.",
    includes: ["Three hook variants (1A/1B/1C) to test", "Beats from the menu: On-ramp, Problem, Agitation, Demonstration, Proof, Trust Builder (risk reversal), CTA, Outro", "One capture per scene: A-roll and B-roll are separate numbered scenes", "Per scene: Script, On-screen text, Visual"],
    worked: `<table><thead><tr><th>Scene</th><th>Section</th><th>Script (spoken)</th><th>On screen</th><th>Visual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1A</td><td>Hook A</td><td>\u201cWatch how much oil this pulls off my face.\u201d</td><td>oily skin? watch this \uD83D\uDC40</td><td>Extreme close-up on the T-zone, roll the stone down, oil lifting onto it.</td></tr><tr><td>1B</td><td>Hook B</td><td>\u201cIf your face gets shiny by noon, you need this.\u201d</td><td>POV: shine-free till 5pm</td><td>Face to camera in daylight, hold the shiny cheek to lens, then the first roll.</td></tr><tr><td>1C</td><td>Hook C</td><td>\u201cI stopped using blotting papers because of this.\u201d</td><td>throw the blotting papers away \uD83D\uDDD1\uFE0F</td><td>Toss a pack of blotting papers out of frame, pick up the roller.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Problem</td><td>\u201cBy lunchtime I'm shiny, my makeup slides, and blotting lifts half of it off.\u201d</td><td></td><td>Face to camera by a window.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Demonstration</td><td>\u201cYou just roll it over the shine. No rubbing, no powder.\u201d</td><td>no powder, no touch-ups \uD83D\uDE4C</td><td>Creator doing one pass across forehead and nose, to camera.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Trust Builder</td><td>\u201cOne roller lasts months, and if it doesn't keep you matte, send it back.\u201d</td><td>washable \u00b7 reusable \u00b7 guaranteed</td><td>Creator rinsing the stone under a tap, holding it back up clean.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>CTA (soft)</td><td>\u201cIf you're still blotting with loo roll at your desk, just give it a go.\u201d</td><td></td><td>Creator to camera, easy shrug, product in hand.</td></tr></tbody></table>`,
    placeholder: `<table><thead><tr><th>Scene</th><th>Section</th><th>Script (spoken)</th><th>On screen</th><th>Visual</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1A</td><td>Hook A</td><td>[hook line one]</td><td>[on-screen text, if it adds a payoff]</td><td>[the single thing filmed, with shot and framing]</td></tr><tr><td>1B</td><td>Hook B</td><td>[hook line two]</td><td>[on-screen text]</td><td>[the single thing filmed]</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Demonstration</td><td>[the words spoken, or blank for B-roll]</td><td>[strategic on-screen text]</td><td>[the single thing filmed]</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Trust Builder</td><td>[the line that removes the risk]</td><td>[reassurance text]</td><td>[what shows the risk reversal]</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>CTA (soft)</td><td>[conversational nudge, not a hard sell]</td><td>[link prompt]</td><td>[creator with product, then a clean product shot]</td></tr></tbody></table>`,
    prompt: "You are writing the Script section for [BRAND NAME or WEBSITE URL], for a [CREATOR TYPE] creator. Brand-led only. Storyboard, scene by scene. Open with three hook variants (1A/1B/1C). Build from the beat menu (On-ramp, Problem, Agitation, Solution Intro, Product Intro, Demonstration, Proof, Trust Builder, CTA, Outro), only the beats needed. Include a risk-reversal under Trust Builder and close soft. Number every scene, one capture each; A-roll and B-roll are separate scenes. Per scene give Script, On-screen text, Visual. No em dashes."
  },

  {
